// src/zen/zen_order.zen
// What order the module walk reaches modules in.
//
// A LEAF, AND ARITHMETIC ON AN INDEX. It imports `std.text` for `str.eq`
// and nothing else — no `Build`, no `Job`, no capability — which is what
// lets both the driver that walks (`zen_build.zen`) and the driver that
// repeats (`zen_run.zen`) stand on it without either standing on the
// other. `zen_path.zen` is the same shape for the same reason: "what a
// module NAME is is a fact about a path".
//
// IT EXISTS FOR ONE GATE, written down here rather than discovered
// later. `tests/determinism/check.sh` check 3 has nothing to vary
// without it: the self-hosted CLI is handed a ROOT and computes the
// module order itself, so the axis that used to shuffle a list of file
// arguments had nowhere to attach. `tests/determinism/README.md`, "the
// shuffle axis", carries the argument for moving it in here rather than
// retiring it.

str = std.text

// THE `i`th MODULE TO REACH, out of `n` — the whole of `--permute`.
// Three fixed permutations and an identity, which are the three
// `check.sh` used to apply to a file list before the CLI stopped taking
// one. Fixed, never random: a gate that fails once and passes on rerun
// teaches you to press rerun.
//
// WHAT IT GUARDS: `gen_c_decl.zen`'s header claims "EVERY LIST IS
// SORTED BY MANGLED NAME ... which is what makes the output independent
// of the order the modules were discovered in, which is the property
// the determinism gate and the fixpoint oracle both stand on". That was
// a claim no gate read. Reordering the walk changes every module index,
// and with it the insertion order into every table sema and gen build.
//
// THE ENTRY IS NEVER PERMUTED and cannot be: the walk queues it before
// this is reached, `emit_program(be, 0, out)` compiles module 0, and
// `root_declares_main` asks module 0 for `main`. Only the modules an
// import reaches move.
//
// It cannot change WHICH modules load either — the reachable set is a
// closure, and a closure does not depend on the order it is computed
// in. Only `n > 0` reaches here, because the caller loops `n` times.
walk_index* = (mode: str, n: usize, i: usize) usize {
    mode.eq("reverse").match({
        true  => n - 1 - i,
        false => mode.eq("rotate").match({
            true  => (i + 1) % n,
            false => mode.eq("interleave").match({
                true  => interleaved(n, i),
                false => i,
            }),
        }),
    })
}

// Evens then odds: the odd indices first, then the even ones. It is the
// one of the three that a descending sort does not also produce, which
// is why three permutations are checked and not one.
interleaved = (n: usize, i: usize) usize {
    half = n / 2;
    (i < half).match({
        true  => i * 2 + 1,
        false => (i - half) * 2,
    })
}
